"""Editorial question designs for journalism and television history."""

from review_angle_plans import audit, revise_domain


JOURNALISM = """history_origins|How a News Outlet Began|Which audience, ownership, and technology shaped one outlet's founding?|Compare charters, early issues, funding, and local context.
famous_examples|A Landmark Investigation Revisited|What did one celebrated report verify at publication time?|Read original documents, sourcing notes, corrections, and later outcomes.
hidden_stories|The Workers Behind the Byline|Whose research, translation, local reporting, or production work is uncredited?|Trace assignments, archives, and attributed staff accounts.
beginner_explainers|How a Story Is Verified|What checks turned one tip into a publishable news claim?|Follow source identification, independent confirmation, context, and editor review.
advanced_concepts|Independence and Ownership|How might ownership or funding influence a newsroom's choices?|Compare governance, disclosed interests, coverage patterns, and staff testimony.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|A Source Claim Still Disputed|Which parts of one historical report remain unresolved?|Compare primary records, conflicting witnesses, corrections, and missing evidence.
technology_innovation|A New Distribution Channel|How did one platform alter reporting, reach, and correction speed?|Compare archives, audience data, newsroom routines, and platform dependence.
people_personalities|The Editor's Decision|What alternatives did a named editor face on a contested story?|Use dated notes and published record without guessing motives.
major_events|A Crisis Tests the Newsroom|How did one outlet verify information during a dated emergency?|Trace publishing timeline, source quality, corrections, and public impact.
how_it_works|From Tip to Correction|How does a newsroom handle a claim from first contact through follow-up?|Map verification, legal review, publication, reader feedback, and remedy.
rise_fall|Why a News Outlet Closed|Which economic and political pressures ended one newsroom?|Compare revenue, ownership, audience, regulation, and staff accounts.
then_vs_now|Reporting the Same Beat Across Eras|How did one beat's sources, speed, and access change?|Compare dated stories and production constraints, not nostalgic claims.
future_developments|A Proposed Newsroom Model|What evidence would show whether a membership or public model sustains coverage?|Define finances, editorial independence, reach, and accountability.
myths_vs_facts|The Scoop Legend Checked|Does a famous first-to-report claim match publication records?|Compare timestamps, editions, source documents, and later retellings.
documentary_stories|One Story's Full Record|How did a published investigation evolve through challenge and correction?|Build a dated chain of documents, editions, responses, and outcomes.
lived_experience|Living Through a Report|How did one affected person experience a news story?|Use consented account with the original report and documented follow-up.
institutional_pathway|A Correction Request|How can a reader challenge a factual error at one outlet?|Trace submission, review, decision, display, and archive update.
incentives_norms|Speed Versus Verification|How did deadlines and metrics affect one newsroom decision?|Compare policy, actual workflow, errors, and editor explanations.
access_and_exclusion|Who Could Read the News?|Which language, pay, disability, or distribution barriers limited one outlet?|Compare formats, pricing, geography, and audience evidence.
community_variation|One Event, Several Newsrooms|How did local and national outlets frame the same event differently?|Compare sources, audience, timing, and correction record.
policy_in_practice|A New Editorial Rule|Did a sourcing or disclosure rule change published practice?|Compare samples before and after, with enforcement records.
survey_vs_story|Trust Polls and Reader Accounts|What can one news-trust survey establish about a community?|Check sampling, wording, outlet specificity, and qualitative accounts.
power_and_voice|Who Gets Quoted?|Whose views dominate one issue's coverage?|Code sources by role and compare with affected-group representation.
unintended_consequence|A Report's Secondary Effect|Did one investigation create an unforeseen harm or remedy?|Trace documented consequences without assuming causation from timing alone.
comparative_setting|Two Outlets, One Claim|Why did two newsrooms reach different conclusions?|Compare evidence, verification standards, publication date, and corrections.
change_over_time|A Beat Loses Reporters|What coverage changed after staffing contracted?|Compare story volume, subjects, original sourcing, and community needs.
ethical_dilemma|Naming a Vulnerable Source|How did a newsroom weigh public interest against one person's safety?|Examine consent, alternatives, harm, and policy without revealing identity.
field_source_verification|Following a Citation to Origin|Does a reported claim rest on the cited primary document?|Trace exact wording, context, date, and any missing link.
field_story_framing|The Lead Changes the Story|How do two headlines frame the same verified facts?|Compare claim accuracy, omitted context, and reader understanding.
field_audience_measurement|Who Actually Saw the Report?|Do reach metrics represent unique readers or meaningful engagement?|Check denominator, platform duplication, and sampling.
field_platform_incentive|When the Feed Shapes Coverage|How did platform distribution rules affect one editorial choice?|Compare traffic, incentives, decisions, and independence safeguards.
field_editorial_correction|A Correction's Visibility|Was a corrected error easy for affected readers to find?|Compare original placement, update notice, syndication, and archive status."""


TELEVISION = """history_origins|The First Years of a TV Service|Which technology, policy, and audience shaped one early broadcaster?|Compare licenses, schedules, equipment, and surviving recordings.
famous_examples|A Landmark Broadcast in Context|Why did one program matter at the time?|Compare contemporary reviews, ratings methods, competitors, and later memory.
hidden_stories|Crew Beyond the Credits|Whose camera, sound, editing, design, and clerical work built one show?|Use production files and attributed crew accounts.
beginner_explainers|How a TV Episode Was Made|Which stages took one program from script to broadcast?|Follow a dated production with budget, roles, and recording format.
advanced_concepts|Broadcast Form and Constraint|How did live or recorded production shape one show's pacing and style?|Compare scripts, equipment, rehearsal, editing, and actual episode.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|A Missing Television Episode|What can be reconstructed when one broadcast recording is lost?|Compare scripts, listings, photos, reviews, and archive gaps.
technology_innovation|A Format Change On Screen|What changed when one broadcaster adopted color, videotape, or digital production?|Compare costs, visual choices, workflow, and audience access.
people_personalities|Who Shaped the Series?|How did writers, directors, performers, and producers divide creative authority?|Compare contracts, scripts, credits, and interviews.
major_events|Television During a Public Event|How did one broadcaster cover a dated event and revise its account?|Compare live footage, editorial decisions, later corrections, and archive.
how_it_works|From Schedule to Screen|How did one program move through commissioning, production, transmission, and preservation?|Trace the institutions and contracts at each step.
rise_fall|Why a Program Ended|Which evidence explains one show's cancellation or revival?|Compare cost, schedule, audience measurement, contracts, and executive records.
then_vs_now|One Genre Across TV Eras|How did the same genre change with production and distribution?|Compare matched programs, formats, budgets, and audience contexts.
future_developments|Preserving Born-Digital Television|What must an archive retain for future study of one digital series?|Examine masters, metadata, rights, versions, and access plans.
myths_vs_facts|The First-Ever TV Claim|Does a pioneering claim depend on a narrow definition of television?|Compare dates, technology, geography, and surviving documentation.
documentary_stories|A Broadcast's Surviving Paper Trail|What can one episode's files reveal beyond the recording?|Build a dated record of script, production, transmission, response, and reuse.
creative_brief|Why This Program Was Commissioned|Which audience and public or commercial goals shaped one series?|Read original brief, budget, scheduling, and delivered episodes.
materials_and_tools|The Studio's Technical Limits|How did cameras, lighting, tape, or sets constrain one production?|Compare equipment records with visual results, without treating limits as flaws.
process_breakdown|One Scene From Script to Air|Which changes occurred between draft, rehearsal, recording, and broadcast?|Compare surviving versions and crew decisions.
composition_choice|What the Camera Emphasized|How did framing or editing shape interpretation of one scene?|Analyze shot sequence and contemporary context, distinguishing intent from effect.
collaborative_authorship|Credit on a TV Episode|Who contributed to a major creative choice?|Compare scripts, production notes, contracts, and conflicting memories.
adaptation_across_media|Story on Page and Screen|What changed when one work became a television program?|Compare rights, structure, performance, and audience reception.
audience_reception|How Viewers Responded|What can ratings, letters, and reviews actually establish?|Check measurement era, sample, and differences among audiences.
cultural_context|A Program in Its Society|How did one show reflect or contest norms of its broadcast period?|Use original episodes and contemporary public response.
preservation_restoration|Saving a TV Recording|What choices preserve one fragile recording and its context?|Review carrier, transfer, metadata, versions, and documentation.
rights_and_attribution|Who Can Reuse the Broadcast?|Which rights and credits govern access to one archived episode?|Check local copyright, performer and music agreements, and archive terms.
style_comparison|Two TV Versions of One Idea|How do visual, sound, and schedule choices change the same format?|Compare bounded episodes under their production constraints.
creative_failure|An Ambition the Production Missed|Why did one episode fall short of its makers' stated aim?|Compare brief, budget, surviving cut, reviews, and later accounts.
field_textual_form|Reading a Teleplay|How does one script signal timing, images, and performance?|Compare page directions with surviving broadcast.
field_performance_choice|A Performance on Camera|How did one actor's delivery interact with framing and editing?|Analyze recorded scene and credited choices without invented intent.
field_audience_history|Who Watched When?|How did availability and audience composition change over a program's life?|Compare schedules, access, ratings methods, and later streaming data.
field_translation_or_adaptation|A Show Across Languages|What changed in one dubbed or localized television version?|Compare scripts, performance, rights, and audience reception.
field_critical_reception|Reviews Then and Later|Why did critical interpretation of one show change?|Compare contemporary reviews with later scholarship and new archives."""


if __name__ == "__main__":
    revise_domain("122", "122_journalism_media_history", JOURNALISM,
                  "Journalism and media history through original reporting, verification, institutions, ownership, audiences, corrections, and documented public effects.",
                  "dated original stories and editions, newsroom records, primary source documents, ownership and audience data, correction archives, and consented reporter and community accounts",
                  "Name outlet, story, jurisdiction, audience, and date. Verify exact claims against original records, protect vulnerable sources, and distinguish reach from trust or public effect.")
    revise_domain("123", "123_television_history", TELEVISION,
                  "Television history through broadcast technology, commissioning, collaborative production, surviving programs, audiences, rights, and preservation.",
                  "surviving broadcasts and scripts, broadcaster schedules and production files, contemporary reviews, archive metadata, rights records, and attributed crew accounts",
                  "Name program, broadcaster, country, episode, and date. Distinguish contemporary reception from later reputation, mark missing recordings, and verify reuse rights.")
    rows = audit()
    print(f"Plans: {len(rows)}; reviewed question designs: {sum(r['status'] == 'editorial_question_reviewed' for r in rows)}")
